{"page":509,"blocks":[{"x":0.72177,"y":0.05257,"w":0.22974,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"8.7 Numerical Integration 495"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.09345,"w":0.58882,"h":0.02814,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"presentation we assume that ƒ is positive, but the only requirement is for it to be continu- ous over the interval of integration 3a, b4 ."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.14553,"w":0.21022,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Trapezoidal Approximations"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.17018,"w":0.59308,"h":0.07418,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"The Trapezoidal Rule for the value of a definite integral is based on approximating the region between a curve and the x-axis with trapezoids instead of rectangles, as in Figure 8.7. It is not necessary for the subdivision points x0, x1, x2, . . . , xn in the figure to be evenly spaced, but the resulting formula is simpler if they are. We therefore assume that the length of each subinterval is"},{"x":0.61254,"y":0.26206,"w":0.08405,"h":0.02065,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"∆x = b - a"},{"x":0.67216,"y":0.26994,"w":0.03023,"h":0.01695,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"n ."},{"x":0.76443,"y":0.31179,"w":0.04306,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = f(x)"},{"x":0.51014,"y":0.33349,"w":0.07812,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Trapezoid area"},{"x":0.51449,"y":0.34495,"w":0.07285,"h":0.01713,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"(y1 + y2)Δx 1 2"},{"x":0.55204,"y":0.42848,"w":0.21406,"h":0.01215,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"y1 y2 yn−1"},{"x":0.78003,"y":0.42848,"w":0.01072,"h":0.01215,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"yn"},{"x":0.49236,"y":0.47901,"w":0.06172,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"x0 = a x1"},{"x":0.58007,"y":0.47954,"w":0.10825,"h":0.0203,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"x2 Δx"},{"x":0.72526,"y":0.47954,"w":0.07123,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"xn−1 xn = b"},{"x":0.48449,"y":0.51084,"w":0.34226,"h":0.07366,"fs":0.01151,"n":6,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 8.7 The Trapezoidal Rule approximates short stretches of the curve y = ƒ(x) with line segments. To approximate the integral of ƒ from a to b, we add the areas of the trapezoids made by joining the ends of the segments to the x-axis."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.59984,"w":0.59294,"h":0.03858,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The length ∆x = (b - a)>n is called the step size or mesh size. The area of the trapezoid that lies above the ith subinterval is"},{"x":0.54687,"y":0.65356,"w":0.08835,"h":0.02321,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"∆xayi-1 + yi"},{"x":0.60174,"y":0.65356,"w":0.08835,"h":0.03044,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"2 b = ∆x"},{"x":0.67609,"y":0.66331,"w":0.09197,"h":0.02068,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2 (yi-1 + yi),"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.69401,"w":0.58812,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"where yi-1 = ƒ(xi-1) and yi = ƒ(xi). (See Figure 8.7.) The area below the curve y = ƒ(x) and above the x-axis is then approximated by adding the areas of all the trapezoids:"},{"x":0.49858,"y":0.73498,"w":0.04173,"h":0.01982,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"T = 1"},{"x":0.53213,"y":0.73498,"w":0.25641,"h":0.02703,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"2 (y0 + y1)∆x + 1 2 (y1 + y2)∆x + g"},{"x":0.53245,"y":0.76695,"w":0.02524,"h":0.01982,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"+ 1"},{"x":0.54951,"y":0.76695,"w":0.25288,"h":0.02703,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"2 (yn-2 + yn-1)∆x + 1 2 (yn-1 + yn)∆x"},{"x":0.51506,"y":0.79999,"w":0.0596,"h":0.02321,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= ∆x a1"},{"x":0.56648,"y":0.79999,"w":0.2482,"h":0.03044,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"2 y0 + y1 + y2 + g+ yn-1 + 1 2 ynb"},{"x":0.51505,"y":0.839,"w":0.03687,"h":0.01999,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= ∆x"},{"x":0.53792,"y":0.8462,"w":0.2722,"h":0.02068,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2 (y0 + 2y1 + 2y2 + g+ 2yn-1 + yn),"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.87757,"w":0.03997,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"where"},{"x":0.42351,"y":0.89991,"w":0.4676,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":0,"t":"y0 = ƒ(a), y1 = ƒ(x1), . . . , yn-1 = ƒ(xn-1), yn = ƒ(b)."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.9236,"w":0.48596,"h":0.01281,"fs":0